# ============================================================================
# ahOntology.ttl — STATUS: FROZEN as design-reference (2026-07-12, #322)
#
# This is the DESIGN ontology (what ahOS is made of). Per ADR-036 §4 the DATABASE
# is becoming canonical and this .ttl becomes a generated seed-in / export-out
# projection once the registry lands (#326). Until then it is FROZEN: no new
# speculative growth. vision.md parks the OWL/RDF machinery as "not now"
# (Additive, not re-architecture) — honoured here.
#
# Load-bearing content stays in the main body. Speculative / not-yet-realised
# branches are parked in APPENDIX A at the end of this file (Product / Outcome /
# KPI / PreservationLevel / AuditEvent), clearly marked and out of the main flow.
# ============================================================================

@prefix ahos:    <http://example.com/ahos-ontology#> .
@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix schema:  <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix foaf:    <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
# NOTE (ADR-033 / #192): skos: is pulled in ahead of its own vocabulary-node
# issue (#195) because skos:closeMatch is the cross-vocabulary mapping
# predicate used by every adoption in this file, not just the Areas-hierarchy
# edges #195 introduces. prov: landed with #193's block; dcterms: lands with
# that same block (ADR-033 / #196). foaf: lands here (ADR-033 / #194) — this
# was the QA nit from PR #201's round 1: ADR-021 shipped `foaf:` CURIEs in
# vocab.ts (RELATIONSHIP_VOCABULARY) months before this prefix caught up.

# =====================================================================
# VOCAB GUIDE (the three layers + the two axes)
# =====================================================================
# Every node in this ontology carries TWO orthogonal tags so it can be
# filtered along either axis:
#
#   - ahos:level — semantic role (meta / ontology / instance)
#   - ahos:inBox — strict W3C split  (TBox / ABox)
#
# The HTML viewer uses both: a level toggle and a box toggle, combinable.
#
# | Vocab    | Provides                                                  | Used for                                           |
# | -------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
# | owl:     | the meta tools — Thing, Class, ObjectProperty, …          | declaring what kind of building block something is |
# | rdfs:    | the relation tools — subClassOf, label, domain, range     | linking blocks together                            |
# | schema:  | (schema.org) domain concepts — Person, Organization, …    | the things in the world                            |
# | ahos:    | your coined concepts — Entity, KnowledgeFramework, …      | the things specific to your ontology               |
#
# ahos:level values (by ROLE in the self-description):
#   - "meta"     : the self-documenting layer — the ontology talking about itself.
#                  Includes ahOntology (the document), ahos:level and ahos:inBox
#                  (the tagging properties), and the external standard vocab we rely
#                  on (owl:Thing, rdfs:Class, owl:Ontology, schema:Person).
#   - "ontology" : "what types of things exist, and what do they show" — your coined
#                  classes (Entity, KnowledgeFramework, Category, …) and properties
#                  (hasCategory, partOf, …), AND the Category individuals that ARE the
#                  vocabulary (Projects, Areas, People, skos:Concept, …) — things get
#                  typed `a ahos:Projects`, so their ROLE is schema even though they sit
#                  in the ABox. Mostly TBox, but not all (see the Category note below).
#   - "instance" : "here is the data" — concrete individuals typed against the classes
#                  (PARA, ahOS, angus, example-project, …) — leaves that nothing is
#                  typed against. The ABox.
#
# ahos:inBox values (strict W3C TBox/ABox split):
#   - "TBox" : the schema — anything declared `a owl:Class`, `a owl:*Property`, or
#              `a owl:Ontology`.
#   - "ABox" : the data — every other named individual.
#
# The two axes DECORRELATE — they are not the same distinction wearing two names.
# Mapping every tagged term into the 2×3 grid (box × level) shows five populated
# cells and one that is structurally empty:
#
#              | level: meta        | level: ontology     | level: instance
#   ---------- | ------------------ | ------------------- | ------------------
#   box: TBox  | header + tag props | coined classes +    | ∅  (a schema class
#              | + design classes   | properties          |     that is itself
#              | (Boundary, …)      | (the schema proper) |     concrete data —
#              |                    |                     |     cannot exist)
#   box: ABox  | TypeAxis, the      | the Category        | angus, ahOS, PARA,
#              | Boundary/Limitation| individuals         | personal, drives,
#              | individuals        | (Projects, People,  | example-projects —
#              | (system-describing | skos:Concept, …)    | leaves nothing is
#              | facts)             |                     | typed against
#
# The off-diagonal cells are the point. ABox×meta is long-standing: RepoOrganisation-
# Boundary is `a ahos:Boundary` (an individual → ABox) but its ROLE is describing the
# system → meta. The Category-as-metaclass pattern (ahos:Category is owl:Class AND
# rdfs:subClassOf rdfs:Class) puts its instances — ahos:Projects, ahos:Areas, … — in
# ABox×ontology: they are individuals (ABox) whose ROLE is vocabulary (ontology),
# because things get typed `a ahos:Projects`. This mirrors how EVERY metaclass here
# treats its instances — an instance keeps its metaclass's LEVEL and only flips the
# BOX to ABox (Boundary→its individuals stay meta; Category→its individuals stay
# ontology). The discriminator for level is ROLE, tested by "does anything get typed
# `a X`?": yes for Categories (vocabulary → ontology), no for PARA/angus (leaf → the
# instance). inBox stays ABox for all of them (they remain named-individual
# assertions); if you ever want to mark their dual TBox-functional role, inBox is the
# place — but that is a separate call from level. (Ratified 2026-07-12, ADR-036 §2.)
#
# Note: ahOntology is TYPED as an individual (a owl:Ontology), but its ROLE is meta —
# it documents the system, it isn't a piece of the data.

# =====================================================================
# DESIGN: TWO-AXIS MODEL FOR INDIVIDUALS (type ⊥ domain)
# =====================================================================
# Every individual that represents "a thing in the world" carries two
# orthogonal pieces of metadata:
#
#   - rdf:type        → WHAT KIND of thing it is (e.g. a Projects, a Tasks)
#   - ahos:hasDomain  → WHICH SPHERE of activity it belongs to (work, personal)
#
# Categories are SHARED across frameworks where the concept genuinely aligns
# (Projects, Tasks are universal — both PARA and work-team-os hasCategory
# them). Framework-specific categories exist only where the concept truly
# differs (Areas/Resources/Archive are PARA-only; Epics is work-only).
#
# This separation is DESIGNED TO support three crisp query patterns — NOT yet realised
# in the runtime graph (entities are typed by a string entity_type column, not
# rdf:type ahos:Projects, and there is no stored domain axis on entity rows). See
# ahos:TwoAxisQueryLimitation below; tracked by #312 / ADR-036 (the accepted design that
# closes this by construction) and #315 (as-of queries):
#
#   "all projects"          → ?x rdf:type ahos:Projects
#   "everything work"    → ?x ahos:hasDomain ahos:work
#   "all work projects"  → ?x rdf:type ahos:Projects AND ?x ahos:hasDomain ahos:work
#
# Why two axes instead of one? An earlier design folded both into per-framework
# categories (HLProjects, HLTasks, PersonalTasks). That made "all projects
# across domains" an awkward UNION query. The current model unifies on type,
# and lets domain carry the framework-affiliation as a separate orthogonal
# axis. Extending to a new team (e.g. Company-B with UserStories): add their
# framework + a new domain individual + framework-specific categories where
# concepts truly differ. The same three-query pattern would then work — once the
# two-axis model is realised in the runtime (see ahos:TwoAxisQueryLimitation).
#
# The design is also encoded structurally in the graph (not just here in prose):
# see ahos:TypeAxis, ahos:DomainAxis (instances of ahos:DesignAxis), and the
# ahos:partOfAxis edges from ahos:Category, ahos:Domain, ahos:hasDomain.
# Graph queries can discover the design directly.

# =====================================================================
# ONTOLOGY HEADER
# =====================================================================

ahos:ahOntology a owl:Ontology, ahos:Entity, ahos:DesignOntology ;
    rdfs:label "ahOntology" ;
    rdfs:comment """The ahOS design ontology — a description of what ahOS is made of.
    Self-referential: this ontology contains a node for itself, related by ahos:describes
    to the OS it describes.

    Typed as an ahos:DesignOntology to make the distinction explicit: this TTL is the
    SCHEMA, not the runtime entity graph. The runtime side (ahos:ahRuntimeOntology) is
    a placeholder here; the actual runtime data lives in artifacts / entities /
    artifact_external_links when ahOS is operating. (Earlier drafts paired this
    TTL with an `ahDataExample.ttl` instance-data sibling; that empty placeholder
    was dropped on 2026-06-16 — examples will live inline when needed.)

    Two parallel hierarchies:

    (1) THE TYPE HIERARCHY (instances -> classes -> owl:Thing):
        Concrete individuals like ahos:angus or ahos:PARA are linked by
        'a' (rdf:type) up through their classes to the root ahos:Entity,
        and ultimately owl:Thing. This is the 'what is this a kind of' axis.

    (2) THE VOCABULARY-TERM HIERARCHY (the meta level):
        The terms themselves are classified by what kind of vocabulary
        element they are: classes are typed owl:Class, properties are typed
        owl:ObjectProperty or owl:DatatypeProperty. This is the 'what kind of
        building block is this' axis, and it sits orthogonal to (1).""" ;
    dcterms:creator ahos:angus ;
    ahos:describes ahos:ahOS, ahos:ahRuntimeOntology ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-06-15"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:dateModified "2026-07-12"^^xsd:date ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .  # typed as an individual but plays a meta role — it documents the system. Strict W3C: an owl:Ontology declaration is TBox.

# =====================================================================
# AXIS 2: VOCABULARY TERMS (the meta level — TBox)
# Classes are 'a owl:Class'. Properties are 'a owl:ObjectProperty' /
# 'a owl:DatatypeProperty'. Annotation property ahos:level tags each.
# =====================================================================

# --- The self-describing tag ---

ahos:level a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "level" ;
    rdfs:comment """Semantic-role tag for filtering nodes by what they are ABOUT. Orthogonal to ahos:inBox (which is the mechanical W3C TBox/ABox split): the level axis tracks where the node sits in the abstraction hierarchy, NOT class-vs-individual.

Three levels:

  | Level    | Subject matter                                  | Examples                                                                                              |
  |----------|-------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
  | meta     | Facts about the ontology / modelling layer      | ahos:AccessControlLimitation, ahos:RepoOrganisationBoundary, ahos:ahRuntimeOntology, ahos:ahOntology  |
  |          | itself                                          | (and the meta-classes: ahos:Boundary, ahos:KnownLimitation, ahos:DesignOntology, ahos:RuntimeOntology)|
  | ontology | Design vocabulary — facts that ARE the          | ahos:MapDontStore (principle), ahos:R1UniversalAccess (requirement),                                  |
  |          | rule-book describing ahOS                       | ahos:Q5TopicDetectionSignal (open question), ahos:B1PR25vsPR59 (design contradiction)                 |
  | instance | Real-world things the system tracks             | ahos:angus, ahos:googleDrive, ahos:captureSkill, ahos:notionDailyLogDBPersonal                        |

The key distinction the level axis carries (that the W3C TBox/ABox split CANNOT carry): ahos:AccessControlLimitation and ahos:angus are both ABox in W3C terms — both are individual assertions, the mechanics are identical — but they live at different semantic levels. The first is a fact ABOUT the ontology's coverage of the system; the second is a fact IN the data the ontology tracks. The level tag is what separates them.

Class definitions are always TBox; their level depends on what they describe. Individual assertions are always ABox; their level depends on what THEY are about. The two axes are independent.""" ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:inBox a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "in box" ;
    rdfs:comment """Strict W3C TBox / ABox split — mechanical, not interpretive. Values:

  - 'TBox' for vocabulary triples: anything declared `a owl:Class`, `a owl:*Property`, or `a owl:Ontology`. These define what KINDS of things can exist and how they relate.
  - 'ABox' for individual assertions: anything where the subject is a named individual being asserted as a class member or having properties asserted on it. These are the FACTS — specific things, specific values.

Orthogonal to ahos:level. Both ahos:angus and ahos:AccessControlLimitation are ABox (mechanical W3C call); they differ on level (instance vs meta — what they're about). Conversely, ahos:Principle and ahos:Boundary are both TBox (class definitions); they differ on level (ontology vs meta — the rule-book layer they describe). The two axes encode different distinctions and decorrelate freely.""" ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Root class ---

ahos:Entity a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf owl:Thing ;
    rdfs:label "Entity" ;
    rdfs:comment "The root of the AHOS vocabulary. Everything in this ontology is ultimately an Entity." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Classes (all descend from ahos:Entity) ---

ahos:KnowledgeFramework a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge Framework" ;
    rdfs:comment "A system for organising or classifying knowledge, e.g. PARA, GTD, work-team-os." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Category a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity, rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Category" ;
    rdfs:comment "A classification bucket defined by a framework. Categories are themselves classes (a metaclass): saying ahos:Projects a ahos:Category means Projects is both a Category instance AND a class that things can be typed against. So 'ahos:project-a a ahos:Projects' works directly — no separate Project class needed. ALSO: Category enables the TYPE axis of the two-axis individual design (see DESIGN block at top). See ahos:partOfAxis ahos:TypeAxis below. W3C NOTE: because ahos:Category is an owl:Class AND (via rdfs:subClassOf rdfs:Class) a metaclass, this is OWL 2 Full, not DL — no OWL-DL reasoner will load the ontology. Acceptable: the consumers are LLMs and graph traversal, not a DL reasoner. (This is metamodelling — a class whose instances are themselves classes — NOT DL 'punning', which is the distinct technique of giving one IRI independent class-meaning and individual-meaning; do not describe it as punning.)" ;
    ahos:partOfAxis ahos:TypeAxis ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:KnowledgeStore a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge Store" ;
    rdfs:comment "A place where knowledge is stored, e.g. a Google Drive." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:OperatingSystem a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Operating System" ;
    rdfs:comment "A personal/team operating system: the overarching system tying frameworks and stores together." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Domain a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Domain" ;
    rdfs:comment "A sphere of activity / context that uses a specific framework to organise itself. e.g. ahos:personal (uses PARA), ahos:work (uses work-team-os). ALSO: Domain enables the DOMAIN axis of the two-axis individual design (see DESIGN block at top) — orthogonal to the TYPE axis carried by Category." ;
    ahos:partOfAxis ahos:DomainAxis ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Design pattern: the two-axis model encoded as a graph structure ---

ahos:DesignAxis a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Design Axis" ;
    rdfs:comment "An orthogonal axis of metadata in the two-axis individual design. Currently: TypeAxis (rdf:type → Category) and DomainAxis (ahos:hasDomain → Domain). Together they let individuals be queried by 'what kind' and 'which sphere' independently. See DESIGN block at the top of this file." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Design vs Runtime ontology distinction (branch O of version A) ---
# Two kinds of ontology operate in ahOS, and they're easy to conflate:
# the design ontology is the SCHEMA (this TTL); the runtime ontology is
# the live entity GRAPH that ahOS maintains in the substrate when it's
# operating (artifacts / entities / artifact_external_links rows,
# organised by PARAMPS). Same shapes, different roles. Folded in
# explicitly so the distinction has a node in the ontology itself.

ahos:DesignOntology a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Design Ontology" ;
    rdfs:comment "A description of what ahOS *is made of* — the classes, properties, individuals, principles, and decisions that shape the system. Distinct from an ahos:RuntimeOntology (what ahOS *tracks*): a design ontology is the schema; runtime is the data populated against that schema. This TTL (ahos:ahOntology) is the canonical instance. Self-referential by design: a design ontology contains a node for itself (this class). Folded in from branch O of version A." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:RuntimeOntology a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Runtime Ontology" ;
    rdfs:comment "The entity graph ahOS *tracks* at runtime — the live data in the artifacts / entities / artifact_external_links tables, organised at the framework level by PARAMPS (per ADR-013 + ADR-018). Distinct from an ahos:DesignOntology (the schema): runtime is the data, design is the shape. Not populated in this TTL; ahos:ahRuntimeOntology is the individual standing in for it here. (Earlier drafts paired this with an `ahDataExample.ttl` instance-data sibling; that empty placeholder was dropped on 2026-06-16.) Folded in from branch O of version A." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Boundary a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Boundary (what this ontology does NOT cover)" ;
    rdfs:comment "A first-class declaration of an area of concern that is deliberately outside the scope of this ontology. The ontology self-documents its limits — what's outside is as important to state as what's inside, and an unstated boundary is one a future reader has to infer. Each instance names the excluded area, explains why it's excluded, and (where applicable) points at the artifact that DOES cover it (a different system, a repo convention, an ADR, the codebase itself). One of four sibling classes that together carve the ontology's gap landscape: ahos:Boundary (decided OUT — not the ontology's job), ahos:KnownLimitation (decided IN but acknowledged incomplete — current treatment is partial or coarse), ahos:OpenQuestion (undecided — design call still pending), ahos:Contradiction (tensioned — two positions in conflict awaiting resolution). Introduced at Angus's direction during the J/N/P fold-in pass: A's branch N treated repo-organisational concepts (branches, docs lanes, GitHub Issues) as first-class on a par with substrate vocabulary; B reframes that whole category as a single Boundary instance, and the class itself generalises the pattern for future exclusions." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:KnownLimitation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Known Limitation (acknowledged incomplete coverage)" ;
    rdfs:comment "A first-class declaration of an area that IS within the ontology's scope but where the current treatment is acknowledged to be partial, coarse, or known to break down. Sibling to ahos:Boundary — the distinguishing question is decided-out vs decided-in-but-incomplete: a Boundary is not the ontology's job (covered elsewhere or not at all); a KnownLimitation is the ontology's job and we've chosen to accept a weaker model than the domain deserves. Sibling to ahos:OpenQuestion — the distinguishing question is undecided vs decided-but-weak: an OpenQuestion has an active design call pending; a KnownLimitation is settled (the current weak treatment IS the decision, for now). Sibling to ahos:Contradiction — a Contradiction is two positions in conflict; a KnownLimitation is one position acknowledged as insufficient. Each instance names the limited area, what's incomplete about the current treatment, why it was accepted (cost / complexity / not-yet-needed), and (where applicable) what closing the gap would look like. Introduced at Angus's direction immediately after the Boundary class — together they let the ontology be honest about both its limits and its weaknesses." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- ahOS system components (#75): three distinct layers ---

ahos:KnowledgeRegistry a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge Registry" ;
    rdfs:comment "The ROUTING layer of ahOS. Knows 'for this kind of content, go here' for ingestion and 'if you want this sort of knowledge, look here' for retrieval. Holds routing rules and direction, NOT bytes. Distinct from ahos:ArtifactsRegister (which is an inventory of stored things) and from ahos:KnowledgeGraph (which holds resolved entities + relations). OPEN QUESTION (#75): confirm there's no subtle duplication with ArtifactsRegister — modelled as distinct but the boundary needs validation against the current Supabase schema." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:KnowledgeGraph a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge Graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "The knowledge ITSELF — resolved entities and their relations. The substrate that retrieval queries against. Distinct from the Registry (routing) and the Artifacts Register (file inventory). Realised today by the Supabase `entities` + `entity_relationships` tables." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:ArtifactsRegister a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:KnowledgeStore ;
    rdfs:label "Artifacts Register" ;
    rdfs:comment "An INVENTORY database — each row says 'this file/thing is here'. Today: the Supabase `artifacts` table. A specialisation of KnowledgeStore (where bytes/data live) — the storage face of the system. Distinct from Knowledge Registry (routing) and Knowledge Graph (resolved knowledge structure)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Capture pathways (#80) ---

ahos:CapturePath a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Capture Path" ;
    rdfs:comment "A way data enters ahOS. Instances: APIPath, ChatInferredPath, ChatCalledPath, MobileUploadPath, WebUploadPath. Each has its own trigger model and default routing." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# [ahos:Product / ahos:Outcome / ahos:KPI / ahos:PreservationLevel classes — moved to APPENDIX A (speculative, parked per #322), 2026-07-12]

# --- Metadata machinery (#76) ---

# [ahos:AuditEvent class — moved to APPENDIX A (speculative, parked per #322), 2026-07-12]
ahos:Status a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Status" ;
    rdfs:comment "A lifecycle status enum. Instances: Pending, Done, Failed, Archived (substrate lifecycle); Open, Resolved (issue / contradiction / question lifecycle, added with branch M fold-in). Applied via ahos:status." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Relation classification (#74) ---

ahos:RelationType a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Relation Type" ;
    rdfs:comment "Classifies an ObjectProperty by HOW it behaves in the graph. Three values: ahos:Relation (generic association — no implied direction or containment), ahos:Hierarchy (containment / classification — implies a tree-like structure), ahos:Flow (directional data movement — what the data-flow view filters to). Tagged onto properties via ahos:relationType. Drives the flow-view rendering (#74)." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Substrate: Database + Table (Supabase substrate) ---

ahos:Database a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:KnowledgeStore ;
    rdfs:label "Database" ;
    rdfs:comment "A structured store organised into Tables. Specialisation of KnowledgeStore. Today the only instance is ahos:supabaseDB (the ahkms Postgres database)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Table a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Table" ;
    rdfs:comment "A relation in a Database. Each instance is one named table (artifacts, entities, entity_relationships, …). Linked to its containing Database via ahos:partOf." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- MCP server + tools ---

ahos:MCPServer a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "MCP Server" ;
    rdfs:comment "A Model Context Protocol server — the API surface agents call into. Exposes a set of MCPTools (capture, search_knowledge, get_artifact, etc.) per ADR-012. Today: one instance, ahos:ahkmsMCPServer (the MCP server in packages/mcp-server/)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:MCPTool a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "MCP Tool" ;
    rdfs:comment "A tool exposed by an MCPServer — a callable function with typed input/output. Distinct from a Capability (skill/agent/command) which is operating-layer; MCPTools are substrate-layer mechanics that capabilities invoke." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Capability framework (per ADR-019) ---

ahos:Capability a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "An invocable operating-layer artifact — a skill, agent, command, or prompt. Per ADR-019, capabilities carry kind (the class), surface (which environment runs them — ahos:Surface enum), scope (which Domain — uses ahos:hasDomain), and host (which repository — uses ahos:hostedBy). Distinct from ahos:MCPTool which is the substrate side." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Skill a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "Skill" ;
    rdfs:comment "A Claude Code skill (markdown + frontmatter under .claude/skills/ or dotclaude/). The kind we have most of today: capture, dos-eod, dos-prep, create-task, etc." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:AgentCapability a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "Agent (capability)" ;
    rdfs:comment "An agent-typed capability per ADR-019. Distinct from ahos:Agent classification by 'kind' attribute. Named with Capability suffix to avoid collision with future ahos:Agent class for AI agents in the runtime sense." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Command a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "Command" ;
    rdfs:comment "A slash-command capability (e.g. `/branch`, `/compact`) — instant, single-purpose, no model loop required." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Prompt a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "Prompt" ;
    rdfs:comment "A reusable prompt template. Less common as a first-class capability but reserved for completeness per ADR-019." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Surface a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Surface" ;
    rdfs:comment "The execution environment a Capability runs in. Per ADR-019 dimensions: claude-code, cowork (claude.ai), api. Modelled as an enum class with named instances." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:DeploymentTarget a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Deployment Target" ;
    rdfs:comment "A concrete runtime location where a Capability's bytes are deployed (synced/installed) for execution. Distinct from ahos:Surface (the *kind* of environment: claude-code | cowork | api). DeploymentTarget answers 'where do the bytes physically go to be invoked' — a per-repo .claude/ directory, the claude.ai cowork app, or (future per ADR-019) the ahkms authenticated serving endpoint. Bridges ahos:hostedBy (source of truth) and ahos:surface (runtime kind) by naming the actual deployment hop." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Six memory-store roles (from dotclaude PR #4 brainstorm) ---

ahos:MemoryRole a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Memory Role" ;
    rdfs:comment "Per the brainstorm cross-mapping from dotclaude PR #4: an abstract role a knowledge-store (or table) plays in the system. A skill names roles, not concrete destinations; user config maps roles → real stores. Six instances: routingRole, mapRole, logRole, stateRole, structuredRole, archiveRole. Many-to-many with stores: a store can play multiple roles, and a role can be realised by multiple stores." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Code repositories (where bytes of code live — dotclaude + ahkms) ---

ahos:CodeRepository a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Code Repository" ;
    rdfs:comment "A git repository containing code or skills. ahOS is built from two: ahkmsRepo (this repo — the substrate) and dotclaudeRepo (capabilities, agent-skills). Per ADR-019, the long-term plan is for ahkms to host owned capabilities directly and index those still synced via Claude cowork." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Artifact + external systems (Tier 1: substrate reality) ---

ahos:Artifact a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Artifact" ;
    rdfs:comment "The base unit of capture — a single 'thing in the system' that came in via a CapturePath. Lives in the artifactsRegister table. OPEN QUESTION (#81): the naming of this base unit is unresolved — alternatives are 'capture' (the act) or 'workflow' (the process). Going with 'Artifact' for now since that's the live Supabase table name, but the name may change when #81 lands. Subclasses (defined later): Atom (synthesis unit), Pointer (registry-mode discovered item)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:ExternalSystem a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "External System" ;
    rdfs:comment "A SaaS / external platform ahOS integrates with. Distinct from a KnowledgeStore — an ExternalSystem is the platform (Notion the app); a KnowledgeStore is a specific container within it (a specific Notion database). KnowledgeStores can ahos:livesIn an ExternalSystem to express the realisation." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:NotionDatabase a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:KnowledgeStore ;
    rdfs:label "Notion Database" ;
    rdfs:comment "A specific database (collection of pages) inside Notion. ahOS uses several: Daily Log (per domain), State Doc (per domain), Tasks, Meetings, Investor CRM. Each is a KnowledgeStore; collectively they realise the working-state side per ADR-013." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Synthesis machinery (Tier 2) — ADR-018 ---

ahos:Atom a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Artifact ;
    rdfs:label "Atom" ;
    rdfs:comment "An atomic Artifact — one event / one thought / one item. Per ADR-018 §2 the unit of capture. The synthesis machinery operates on atoms: ahos:Synthesiser reads atoms, infers workspace+type+domain, bundles them, writes derived products." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Synthesiser a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "Synthesiser" ;
    rdfs:comment "A Capability that reads atoms (via search_knowledge etc.), runs per-atom inference, bundles by domain coherence, writes a derived Product, and marks consumption via artifact_external_links per ADR-013 §7 + ADR-018 §4-7. dos-eod is the canonical synthesiser today." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Discovery machinery (Tier 2) — ADR-016 ---

ahos:Pointer a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Artifact ;
    rdfs:label "Pointer" ;
    rdfs:comment "A registry-mode Artifact: holds a URL/external_id + metadata + content_hash, but NO bytes. Written by the discover loop per ADR-016. May later be promoted to a full Artifact via the enrich path (lifecycle_stage discovered → extracted)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:LifecycleStage a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Lifecycle Stage" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where in the processing pipeline an Artifact sits. Per ADR-016 the canonical progression is discovered → extracted → uploaded → processed. Distinct from ahos:Status (success/fail) — lifecycle is *position*, status is *outcome*. No terminal stage (Retired / Superseded) is currently defined; ahos:NeverForget favours append-only tombstones over hard deletion, but the design of a 'retired' stage value — and whether it applies to atoms, pointers, or both — remains open. See also ahos:Q1ArtifactDerivedLifecycle for the related raw-vs-derived split." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:ReadAdapter a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Read Adapter" ;
    rdfs:comment "A server-side connector that can fetch bytes from an ExternalSystem. Used by the enrich path per ADR-016. Today only Google Drive has one." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Operating layer vs Substrate (Tier 2) — ADR-014 reframing ---

ahos:ArchLayer a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Architectural Layer" ;
    rdfs:comment "Per ADR-014's reframing: ahOS splits into the OPERATING layer (capabilities — skills/agents/commands — the things that USE the system) and the SUBSTRATE layer (tools, tables, stores — the things capabilities act on). This class makes that split queryable." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Pipeline concepts folded in from branches D + G of version A ---

ahos:TopicBoundary a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Topic Boundary" ;
    rdfs:comment "The point at which /capture's bare mode stops walking back through prior conversation: an explicit topic shift, a long pause, or the safety cap (30 messages). Atomic-capture relies on this being detectable — one bare /capture call should produce one atom, not a multi-topic blob. Detection signal is open — see ahos:Q5TopicDetectionSignal. Folded in from branch D of version A." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Bundling a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Bundling (synthesis-time grouping)" ;
    rdfs:comment "A synthesis-time pattern where atoms are grouped by inferred domain coherence (Apple-related atoms together, EHS-related atoms together) before being summarised. Used by dos-eod and the future weekly-reflection / investor-brief consumers — each composes its output from bundles, not from individual atoms. Per-atom inference assigns the domain dimension; bundling reads that dimension and clusters. Folded in from branch G of version A." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:HybridRetrieval a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Hybrid Retrieval (FTS + embeddings)" ;
    rdfs:comment "Retrieval pattern that combines lexical full-text search (already built — Postgres FTS over artifacts) with semantic vector similarity (embeddings — designed only, not built yet). Folded in as a TBox node now because the design contract assumes it; the implementation half is queued. Distinct from search_knowledge today which is FTS-only. Folded in from branch G of version A." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Tier 3 classes: people, content subtypes, correspondence, bindings ---

ahos:Team a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Team" ;
    rdfs:comment "A team / org unit. Distinct from a Domain (which is a sphere of activity); a Team is a group of People who collaborate. e.g. ahos:workTeam." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:ContentSubtype a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Content Subtype" ;
    rdfs:comment "The kind of content an Artifact carries. Stored on artifacts.content_subtype today; promoted here to first-class for queries like 'show me all transcripts'. Instances: Note, Transcript, Conversation, VoiceNote, Image, Handwriting, Letter. The list is intentionally open-ended (brainstorm 2026-06-15, line 163): new dimensions (habit-logs, calendar events, meta-logs) are added by minting a new ContentSubtype instance — no schema change required. Dimensions not yet represented: habit-logs, CalendarEvent, MetaLog (system self-observation)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:DesignConcept a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Design Concept" ;
    rdfs:comment "An ontology-INTERNAL concept: a notion the design ontology itself reasons with (e.g. ahos:ScopePredicate, ahos:HouseStyle, ahos:TheEstate), as opposed to the runtime `concept` entity type (skos:Concept — a subject/topic node in the knowledge graph). Renamed from ahos:Concept 2026-07-12 (#318 finding 9) to remove the skos:Concept name clash, and declared here — its instances previously referenced an undeclared class." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:SourceQuality a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Source Quality" ;
    rdfs:comment "How trustworthy / formal the source of an Artifact is — a classification axis affecting downstream weight, retention value, and the reliability of facts derived from it. Carried by Artifact (and likely by Entity instances that get extracted from artifacts of varying source quality). Open *shape*: the brainstorm hypothesised candidate sub-axes (formality, trustworthiness) but did not commit to one dim or several — see ahos:Q4SourceQualityShape. Also open whether SourceQuality overlaps materially with ahos:capturedVia / ahos:contentSubtype (those answer *where from* and *what kind*; SourceQuality answers *how trustworthy*). No instance values committed yet — adding them is what closing Q4 unlocks. Folded in from branch K of version A. Design-ontology Phase 2 fold (2026-06-15) hypothesised a cross-branch rule: high-formality sources (e.g. a council letter) drive file-for-retention disposition (ahos:Filed); low-formality sources (e.g. a handwritten-note photo) drive archive (ahos:ColdStorage). If the rule holds, branch K (source-quality) and the preservation-level taxonomy collapse via a single mapping — see ahos:Q21SourceQualityDrivesPreservation." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Channel a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Correspondence Channel" ;
    rdfs:comment "An outbound messaging channel for the correspondence edge per ADR-017. e.g. Gmail, LinkedIn post, X post, WhatsApp. Each channel has a SendTool that handles the mechanics." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:SendTool a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:MCPTool ;
    rdfs:label "Send Tool" ;
    rdfs:comment "An MCPTool that handles an outbound send for a specific Channel. Per ADR-017: typed, idempotent sends; credentials handled; append-only provenance. Distinct from substrate tools (capture, search) — Send tools are correspondence-edge tools that can be deleted without affecting the knowledge core." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Principle a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Principle" ;
    rdfs:comment "A load-bearing design principle. Examples: 'map don't store', 'registry not store', 'build opinions rent mechanics', 'capture is dumb' (deferred inference), 'additive not re-architecture'. Anchors ADRs and is referenced by them. Worth modelling so vision.md can cite them by id." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Requirement a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Requirement" ;
    rdfs:comment "A must-do from the brainstorm — what ahOS has to deliver. Distinct from a Principle (load-bearing design rule) and a Decision (ADR establishing a rule). Vision.md cites Requirements by id (R1-R5) and each one traces to the Principles, Decisions, and Capabilities that realise it. Folded in from branch C of the 2026-06-15 design ontology (version A)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:ThesisStatement a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Thesis Statement" ;
    rdfs:comment "A foundational, scope-defining assertion about what ahOS *is*. Distinct from a Principle (a design rule that constrains *how* the system works) and a Requirement (a must-deliver capability). A ThesisStatement anchors the rest of the design: vision.md cites it, principles realise it, requirements are downstream of it. Few in number — the brainstorm thesis ('unified KMS that never forgets, including skills') is the founding example." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Binding a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Binding" ;
    rdfs:comment "Per ADR-019: a named pointer that lets a Capability reference a resource by name (e.g. {{notion.inbox_db}}) and have it resolved at serve-time to the actual identifier for the active scope (personal vs work). One binding per (scope, name) pair. NOT a copy of the value — a pointer into the registry." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:BindingTiming a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Binding Timing" ;
    rdfs:comment "When a Binding is resolved. Per ADR-019: BakeAtServe (v1 — value substituted at serve time, capability ships concrete) vs LateBind (future — resolved at execution time, capability stays templated)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Tier 4 classes (speculative): knowledge tiers, periods, decisions ---

ahos:KnowledgeTier a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge Tier" ;
    rdfs:comment "Importance / value tier from the brainstorm concept 9 (Bronze/Silver/Gold). DISTINCT from PreservationLevel: tier is about IMPORTANCE (how much we care about an artifact); preservation is about HOW LONG we keep it. A Bronze item might still be Permanent; a Gold item might be Filed. Speculative — added per Tier 4." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Period a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Period" ;
    rdfs:comment "A time-bound context. Addresses brainstorm Q2 — when 'home' was MLG vs is now somewhere else. Lets you tag instance-bound facts (council/correspondence belongs to the MLG-era period) so queries against today don't pick up stale state. Speculative — added per Tier 4." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Decision a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Decision" ;
    rdfs:comment "A recorded architectural decision (ADR). Lets the ontology reference *why* a design exists. e.g. ahos:ADR018 ahos:decides ahos:CaptureIsDumb means ADR-018 establishes the dumb-capture principle. Modelled per Tier 4." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:Contradiction a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Contradiction" ;
    rdfs:comment "A contradiction or open tension between two parts of the design, registered in ADR-020. Carries ahos:status (Open / Resolved), ahos:about (what it's about), and optionally ahos:resolvedBy (the Decision that closed it). Distinct from an ahos:OpenQuestion: a Contradiction has two specific things in tension; a Question is underspecified or unanswered (no second pole identified yet). Folded in from branch M of version A." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:OpenQuestion a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Open Question" ;
    rdfs:comment "An unresolved question from the brainstorm — something the design has not yet settled. Carries ahos:status (Open / Resolved) and ahos:about (the thing the question points at). Distinct from an ahos:Contradiction: a Contradiction names two specific things in tension; an OpenQuestion may have only one pole identified, or be asking 'what shape should X take?'. Some OpenQuestions will collapse into Contradictions once the second pole becomes clear; others get answered by a Decision and flip to Resolved. Folded in from branch L of version A." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Properties ---

ahos:hasCategory a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:KnowledgeFramework ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Category ;
    rdfs:label "has category" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a framework to one of its categories." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:organisedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:KnowledgeStore ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:KnowledgeFramework ;
    rdfs:label "organised by" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a knowledge store to the framework structuring it." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# NOTE (ADR-033 / #196): createdBy is adopted DIRECT as dcterms:creator, not
# minted as ahos:createdBy. DCMI declares dcterms:creator as "an entity
# responsible for making the resource" (dcam:rangeIncludes dcterms:Agent,
# owl:equivalentProperty foaf:maker) — our usage (any ahos:Entity -> the
# person who created it) is an exact match, no hedge needed. This is broader
# than the already-adopted `author` -> schema:author (#192), which is scoped
# to People -> Resource/artifact authorship specifically; dcterms:creator
# covers the general ahos:Entity case (e.g. who created this ontology node),
# so the two are NOT double-mapping one relation — different domains, one
# canonical term each. Per the adopt-direct rule this takes the external IRI
# with no ahos: proxy and no skos:closeMatch bridge (annotations attach to
# external terms directly, per the ADR's point 3).
#
# FOAF interplay (ADR-033 / #194, verified against http://xmlns.com/foaf/spec/):
# foaf:maker (rdfs:domain foaf:Thing, rdfs:range foaf:Agent, "an agent that
# made this thing", inverse foaf:made) is the property DCMI's
# owl:equivalentProperty above points at — confirmed on the FOAF side too, no
# conflict. #194 does not mint a THIRD mapping for this relation: dcterms:creator
# stays sole canonical owner in the TTL (already-adopted, broader domain), and
# `made`/`currentProject`'s sibling `foaf:made` stays confined to
# `@ahkms/shared`'s RELATIONSHIP_VOCABULARY as a People<->People-graph term
# (ADR-021) rather than being re-adopted here — one canonical term per
# relation, per the specialist-wins rule this ADR set.

# NOTE (ADR-033 / #192): subject_of is adopted DIRECT as schema:subjectOf, not
# minted as ahos:subjectOf. Schema.org formally declares subjectOf as the
# inverseOf schema:about ("a CreativeWork or Event about this Thing") — since
# `about` is already adopted direct in this file, its declared formal inverse
# is an exact match, not an approximation, so per the adopt-direct rule it
# takes the external IRI with no ahos: proxy and no skos:closeMatch bridge
# (annotations attach to external terms directly, per the ADR's point 3).
# Storage split unchanged: the Postgres edge string is subject_of
# (packages/mcp-server/src/vocab.ts EDGE_TYPES / SCHEMA_ORG_CONTEXT).

ahos:partOf a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "part of" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links an entity to a larger entity it belongs to / is contained in. Broad on purpose — same property works for a project being part of an OS, a task being part of a project, a category being part of a framework." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:describes a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "describes" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a description (typically a document or ontology) to the thing it describes. e.g. ahos:ahOntology ahos:describes ahos:ahOS." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:hasDomain a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Domain ;
    rdfs:label "has domain" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links an entity to the domain it belongs to. e.g. ahos:wedding2026 ahos:hasDomain ahos:personal. CARRIES the DOMAIN axis of the two-axis individual design (orthogonal to rdf:type which carries the TYPE axis). See DESIGN block at top." ;
    ahos:partOfAxis ahos:DomainAxis ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:uses a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Domain ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:KnowledgeFramework ;
    rdfs:label "uses" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a domain to the framework it uses for organisation. e.g. ahos:work ahos:uses ahos:WorkTeamOS." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Design-axis property (encodes which design-axis a vocab term enables) ---

ahos:partOfAxis a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:DesignAxis ;
    rdfs:label "part of axis" ;
    rdfs:comment "Tags an ontology element (class, property) as participating in / enabling a particular design axis. e.g. ahos:Category ahos:partOfAxis ahos:TypeAxis means Category enables the TYPE axis of the two-axis individual design. This property is itself meta — it documents the ontology's design pattern in graph form." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Capture-path property (#80) --- (Outcome/KPI/Product/Preservation properties moved to APPENDIX A)

# [ahos:hasOutcome / produces / producesFor / measures / preservationLevel properties — moved to APPENDIX A (speculative), 2026-07-12]

ahos:capturedVia a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:CapturePath ;
    rdfs:label "captured via" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links an artifact to the capture path that brought it into ahOS. e.g. an artifact ahos:capturedVia ahos:WebUploadPath. (Maps to the existing source_channel field — will integrate when metadata properties are formalised per #76.)" ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Metadata properties (#76) — using standard vocab where it fits ---
#
# Vocab survey notes:
#   - createdAt:   schema:dateCreated   (already used on ahos:ahOS, ahos:ahOntology)
#   - updatedAt:   schema:dateModified  (already used as above)
#   - createdBy:   dcterms:creator      (ADR-033 / #196 — migrated off ahos:createdBy; adopted
#                                        direct, exact match, no ahos: proxy. See NOTE above ahos:partOf.)
#   - lastEditedBy: ahos:lastEditedBy  (kept as an ahos: term — "most recent editor" only
#                                        approximates dcterms:contributor's broader "made a
#                                        contribution" and prov:wasAttributedTo's "responsible
#                                        agent"; per ADR-033's mint+map rule, bridged with
#                                        skos:closeMatch below rather than adopted direct)
#   - source:      ahos:sourceLocation (kept as an ahos: term for symmetry with storageLocation;
#                                        approximates dcterms:source, bridged with skos:closeMatch
#                                        below rather than adopted direct)
#   - tags:        carried inside ahos:metadata (jsonb blob; promote to a Tag class later if needed)

ahos:lastEditedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  schema:Person ;
    rdfs:label "last edited by" ;
    rdfs:comment "Most recent editor. Kept as an ahos: term (not adopted direct) because our meaning only approximates the externals: dcterms:contributor is broader (\"an entity responsible for making contributions to the resource\", not necessarily the LAST one), and prov:wasAttributedTo (domain/range prov:Entity/prov:Agent) is closer in shape but drops the recency nuance. Bridged via skos:closeMatch per ADR-033's mint+map rule." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# ADR-033 / #196: skos:closeMatch bridges promoting the prose-equivalence
# comments above to machine-readable triples (ADR-033 point 2 — mint+map when
# approximate). Non-propagating: this records alignment as queryable data
# without implying inference we don't run.
ahos:lastEditedBy skos:closeMatch dcterms:contributor, prov:wasAttributedTo .

# [ahos:auditHistory property — moved to APPENDIX A (speculative), 2026-07-12]

ahos:metadata a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:label "metadata" ;
    rdfs:comment "Unstructured JSON metadata blob (jsonb at the Postgres layer). Includes the tags list and any other ad-hoc properties not worth promoting to first-class. Stored as a string literal at the RDF level; the consuming application parses as JSON." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:sourceLocation a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:label "source location" ;
    rdfs:comment "URI / path the artifact came from (e.g. Gmail message ID, Drive URL, Notion page ID). Kept as an ahos: term for symmetry with storageLocation (a datatype property; dcterms:source is a related-RESOURCE object property, not a literal URI/path, so it's an approximation rather than an exact match). Bridged via skos:closeMatch per ADR-033's mint+map rule." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:sourceLocation skos:closeMatch dcterms:source .

ahos:storageLocation a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  rdfs:Literal ;
    rdfs:label "storage location" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where the bytes physically live (Supabase Storage bucket path, S3 URI, etc.). Distinct from sourceLocation (where it came from)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:status a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Status ;
    rdfs:label "status" ;
    rdfs:comment "Current lifecycle status. e.g. an artifact ahos:status ahos:Pending." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Relation-type tagging property (#74) ---

ahos:relationType a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "relation type" ;
    rdfs:comment "Tags an ObjectProperty with its behavioural class: ahos:Relation (generic association), ahos:Hierarchy (containment / classification), or ahos:Flow (directional data movement). Used by the HTML viewer's planned flow view (filter edges to relationType=Flow) and by anyone navigating the graph who wants to know 'is this a containment edge or a flow?'. The tagging triples are gathered at the end of the file for compactness." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- External vocabulary adoption (ADR-033) ---
#
# Five external vocabularies are adopted into the knowledge graph: Schema.org
# (#192, this block), PROV-O (#193), FOAF (#194), SKOS (#195), Dublin Core /
# DCTerms (#196). Per ADR-033's adopt-direct rule: reuse an external term's
# IRI directly when its semantics match ours exactly (no ahos: proxy); mint an
# ahos: term + skos:closeMatch bridge only where our meaning is an
# approximation. Each adopted vocabulary gets a node here — an individual
# typed owl:Ontology, carrying the new ahos:usedFor annotation — so "which
# standards does ahOS align to, and for what?" is a graph query
# (`SELECT ?v ?use WHERE { ?v a owl:Ontology ; ahos:usedFor ?use }`), not a
# doc lookup.

ahos:usedFor a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "used for" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which axis of the graph an adopted external vocabulary owns (ADR-033). e.g. schema: owns the spine/default axis; prov: owns lineage; foaf: owns people; skos: owns concept hierarchy; dcterms: owns documents/citation. One canonical term per relation — the specialist vocab takes the relation only when its axis is the point; schema: is the fallback." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

<http://schema.org/> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Schema.org" ;
    ahos:usedFor "Spine / default — general relations; reach for this first. Specialists (PROV-O/FOAF/SKOS/DCTerms) take the relation only when their axis is the point." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

<http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "PROV-O" ;
    ahos:usedFor "Lineage — 'where did this come from?' (wasDerivedFrom, wasGeneratedBy, wasAttributedTo)." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

<http://purl.org/dc/terms/> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "DCTerms" ;
    ahos:usedFor "Documents / citation — creator, references, subject on Resources and artifacts." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

<http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "FOAF" ;
    ahos:usedFor "People — who knows/belongs to whom? Per ADR-021." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

<http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "SKOS" ;
    ahos:usedFor "Concept hierarchy — Areas tree; also supplies the closeMatch mapping predicate." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Substrate / capability / role properties (MCP, DB, skills, dotclaude merge) ---

ahos:exposes a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:MCPServer ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:MCPTool ;
    rdfs:label "exposes" ;
    rdfs:comment "An MCPServer exposes a set of MCPTools. e.g. ahos:ahkmsMCPServer ahos:exposes ahos:captureTool." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:invokes a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Capability ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:MCPTool ;
    rdfs:label "invokes" ;
    rdfs:comment "A Capability invokes one or more MCPTools to do its work. Flow-typed: data flows from skill → tool call. e.g. ahos:captureSkill ahos:invokes ahos:captureTool." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:writesTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:KnowledgeStore ;
    rdfs:label "writes to" ;
    rdfs:comment "A capability or tool writes data into a store. Flow-typed: data movement. e.g. ahos:captureTool ahos:writesTo ahos:artifactsRegister." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:readsFrom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:KnowledgeStore ;
    rdfs:label "reads from" ;
    rdfs:comment "A capability or tool reads data from a store. Flow-typed: data movement. e.g. ahos:searchKnowledgeTool ahos:readsFrom ahos:artifactsRegister." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:playsRole a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:MemoryRole ;
    rdfs:label "plays role" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a store / table / product to the abstract MemoryRole it plays in the brainstorm's six-roles taxonomy. Multi-valued — a store can play multiple roles. e.g. ahos:entitiesTable ahos:playsRole ahos:mapRole, ahos:structuredRole." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:builtFrom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:CodeRepository ;
    rdfs:label "built from" ;
    rdfs:comment "Provenance — entity is built from one or more code repositories. e.g. ahos:ahOS ahos:builtFrom ahos:ahkmsRepo, ahos:dotclaudeRepo." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:hostedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Capability ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:CodeRepository ;
    rdfs:label "hosted by" ;
    rdfs:comment "**Source of truth** — the repository where a Capability's canonical bytes are version-controlled. NOT the runtime location: to be invoked, the bytes must additionally be deployed to a runtime via ahos:syncedTo (per-repo .claude/, claude.ai cowork, or — future — the ahkms authenticated serving endpoint). e.g. ahos:dosEodSkill ahos:hostedBy ahos:dotclaudeRepo. Future state per ADR-019 (Proposed, 2026-06-15): owned capabilities move to ahos:hostedBy ahos:ahkmsRepo — contingent on (a) ADR-019 acceptance AND (b) the authenticated ahkms serving API being built. Until both land, dotclaude remains the canonical home (ADR-019 §Consequences: 'skills are not physically migrated' in the interim)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:syncedTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Capability ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:DeploymentTarget ;
    rdfs:label "synced to" ;
    rdfs:comment "**Runtime location** — where a Capability's bytes are deployed/synced to actually be invoked. Distinct from ahos:hostedBy (source of truth) and ahos:surface (runtime *kind*: claude-code | cowork | api). Names the actual deployment hop: a per-repo .claude/skills/ directory, claude.ai cowork, or the future ahkms auth endpoint. e.g. ahos:correspondenceSkill ahos:syncedTo ahos:perRepoClaudeDir means the bytes are web-fetched from dotclaude into each consuming repo's .claude/skills/ to be invokable at the Claude Code surface. A capability can have multiple syncedTo targets if it deploys to more than one runtime." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:surface a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Capability ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Surface ;
    rdfs:label "surface" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which execution environment a Capability runs in. Per ADR-019: claude-code, cowork (claude.ai), api. e.g. ahos:captureSkill ahos:surface ahos:ClaudeCodeSurface." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Tier 1 properties (substrate reality) ---

ahos:livesIn a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:KnowledgeStore ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:ExternalSystem ;
    rdfs:label "lives in" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a KnowledgeStore to the ExternalSystem it physically lives in. e.g. ahos:notionDailyLogDBPersonal ahos:livesIn ahos:notion, or ahos:personalDrive ahos:livesIn ahos:googleDrive." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# prov:wasDerivedFrom (ADR-033 / #193) replaces the former ahos:derivedFrom —
# mechanical instance-triple rename: both ends are prov:Entity-compatible
# (our ahos:Entity), so the external term is adopted directly, no ahos: proxy.
# House annotation (ahos:relationType) attaches to the external term below,
# per ADR-033 §3 ("house annotations attach to external terms").
# e.g. ahos:task-b prov:wasDerivedFrom ahos:exampleMeetingNote. The primary
# query 'where did this come from?' uses this.

# --- Tier 2 properties ---

ahos:lifecycleStage a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:LifecycleStage ;
    rdfs:label "lifecycle stage" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where an Artifact sits in the processing pipeline. e.g. an Artifact ahos:lifecycleStage ahos:Extracted." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:inLayer a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "in layer" ;
    rdfs:comment "Annotates a class (or instance) with which ArchLayer it sits in. AnnotationProperty so it can apply to classes — e.g. ahos:Capability ahos:inLayer ahos:OperatingLayer." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Tier 3 properties ---

ahos:memberOf a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain schema:Person ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Team ;
    rdfs:label "member of" ;
    rdfs:comment "Person → Team membership. e.g. ahos:angus ahos:memberOf ahos:workTeam." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:contentSubtype a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Artifact ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:ContentSubtype ;
    rdfs:label "content subtype" ;
    rdfs:comment "What kind of content the Artifact carries. Maps to the existing content_subtype column." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:sourceQuality a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Artifact ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:SourceQuality ;
    rdfs:label "source quality" ;
    rdfs:comment "How trustworthy / formal the Artifact's source is, as a classification value. Distinct from ahos:capturedVia (where the artifact came in — agent / programmatic / discovered_<connector>) and ahos:contentSubtype (what kind of content it is). Range values are open — see ahos:Q4SourceQualityShape." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:hasReadAdapter a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:ExternalSystem ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:ReadAdapter ;
    rdfs:label "has read adapter" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links an ExternalSystem to the server-side ReadAdapter that fetches its bytes for the enrich pass (ADR-016). Asserted only where an adapter exists — Drive today. Absence is meaningful (open-world interpretation per ahos:PositiveFactStoreDeriveRest): no triple means no adapter yet, which is the discovery-only state for Notion / Gmail / Slack / GitHub / Calendar. Folded in from branch E of version A." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:sendsVia a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:SendTool ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Channel ;
    rdfs:label "sends via" ;
    rdfs:comment "SendTool → Channel. e.g. ahos:sendEmailTool ahos:sendsVia ahos:gmailChannel." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:bindingScope a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Binding ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Domain ;
    rdfs:label "binding scope" ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope a Binding applies in. e.g. ahos:notionInboxDBBindingPersonal ahos:bindingScope ahos:personal (resolves to a different DB than the work-scoped binding of the same name)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:bindingTiming a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Binding ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:BindingTiming ;
    rdfs:label "binding timing" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the binding is resolved — BakeAtServe (v1) or LateBind (future)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:resolvesTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Binding ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "resolves to" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a Binding's reference token points at. e.g. notionInboxDBBindingPersonal resolves to a specific Notion DB id (the personal one)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# --- Tier 4 properties ---

ahos:tier a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:KnowledgeTier ;
    rdfs:label "tier" ;
    rdfs:comment "Importance tier. e.g. an Artifact ahos:tier ahos:Gold. Distinct from preservationLevel." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:duringPeriod a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Period ;
    rdfs:label "during period" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a state-bound entity to the Period it was true in. e.g. a council-correspondence path during the MLG period." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:decides a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Decision ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "decides" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a Decision establishes / governs. Range is intentionally open (Entity) — a Decision can establish a Principle, change a property semantic, deprecate a class, etc." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:realises a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Requirement, ahos:ThesisStatement ;
    rdfs:label "realises" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links anything that delivers or anchors a Requirement (or ThesisStatement) to that target. Capabilities, substrate tables, and ADRs realise a requirement by implementing it; Principles realise it by being the design rule that makes it safe; substrate classes (e.g. ahos:ArtifactExternalLink) realise a ThesisStatement by being the structural pattern that makes it true. Domain intentionally open. Folded from branch C of version A's edge taxonomy; range expanded for ThesisStatement in chunk 3 of the 2026-06-16 fold." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:about a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "about" ;
    rdfs:comment "Subject-of relation: the linked Entity is what the source is about. Used most heavily by Contradictions and OpenQuestions to point at the thing they're contesting or questioning. Equivalent in role to dcterms:subject; using ahos: for symmetry with the rest of the vocab. Domain and range both open." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

ahos:resolvedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ahos:Contradiction ;
    rdfs:range  ahos:Decision ;
    rdfs:label "resolved by" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a Contradiction to the Decision (ADR) that closed it. Only meaningful for Contradictions with ahos:status ahos:Resolved. Folded from branch M of version A — A's edge taxonomy used 'resolves' as Decision → Contradiction; this is the inverse direction." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "TBox" .

# =====================================================================
# AXIS 1: INDIVIDUALS (the data level — ABox)
# Each block below is a CONCRETE THING, linked by 'a' to its class.
# Follow the 'a' up: individual -> class -> ahos:Entity -> owl:Thing.
# =====================================================================

# --- Design axes (meta — the two-axis individual model encoded in graph form) ---

ahos:TypeAxis a ahos:DesignAxis ;
    rdfs:label "Type Axis" ;
    rdfs:comment "The 'WHAT KIND of thing it is' axis. Realised in the graph via rdf:type pointing at a Category-class. e.g. ahos:project-a rdf:type ahos:Projects. To find all 'projects', query rdf:type ahos:Projects." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

ahos:DomainAxis a ahos:DesignAxis ;
    rdfs:label "Domain Axis" ;
    rdfs:comment "The 'WHICH SPHERE of activity it belongs to' axis. Realised in the graph via ahos:hasDomain pointing at a Domain individual. e.g. ahos:project-a ahos:hasDomain ahos:work. To find 'everything work-flavoured', query ahos:hasDomain ahos:work. Orthogonal to ahos:TypeAxis." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# --- The runtime ontology stand-in (branch O of version A) ---

ahos:ahRuntimeOntology a ahos:RuntimeOntology ;
    rdfs:label "ahOS runtime ontology" ;
    rdfs:comment "Placeholder individual for the runtime entity graph ahOS maintains — the actual data in artifacts / entities / artifact_external_links when the system is operating, organised at the framework level by PARAMPS (the expanded variant of PARA: Project / Area / Resource / Memory / People / Secure / Archive) — though this is the conceptual framing, not the literal type list: the runtime entities.entity_type set today is project/area/resource/people/event/organization/signal/place/concept (Memory/Secure/Archive retired by migration 067 #172; event/organization/signal/place/concept are net-new). Not populated here (this TTL is the design ontology). ahos:ahOntology describes both the system (ahos:ahOS) and this runtime ontology — the design ontology defines what shape the runtime takes. No ahos:organisedBy triple here on purpose: that property is domain-restricted to ahos:KnowledgeStore, and a RuntimeOntology is a graph, not a store of bytes — the PARAMPS framing is captured in prose instead." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# --- Boundaries: what this ontology does NOT cover (J/N/P fold-in) ---
# Each Boundary instance names an area of concern that is deliberately
# outside the ontology's scope, with the rationale and (where applicable)
# a pointer to what DOES cover it. Adding more boundaries as new
# exclusions are identified is encouraged — the catalogue is open.

ahos:RepoOrganisationBoundary a ahos:Boundary ;
    rdfs:label "Boundary: repo organisation" ;
    rdfs:comment "OUT OF SCOPE: how the repo that builds ahOS is organised. Branches (dev / main / experimental/*), documentation lanes (docs/, docs/ADRs/, docs/sessions/, docs/architecture/, docs/architecture/historical/), GitHub Issues used for planning. These describe how the OS is DEVELOPED, not what the OS IS — they're properties of the development workflow, not properties of the system being built. COVERED ELSEWHERE: CONTRIBUTING.md (branch model + PR bar), docs/README.md (docs index + 'where does X go?'), the project's planning convention ('current state → docs/, why → docs/ADRs/, planning → GitHub Issues' from CLAUDE.md). PROVENANCE: A's branch N treated these as first-class concepts on a par with substrate vocabulary; B explicitly reframes the whole category as a single Boundary." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

ahos:ImplementationDetailsBoundary a ahos:Boundary ;
    rdfs:label "Boundary: implementation details" ;
    rdfs:comment "OUT OF SCOPE: specific code files, function signatures, line-level call graphs, internal module structure, language choice for any given component. The ontology operates at the level of capabilities, substrate tables, principles, and decisions — concrete code is the realisation of those, not the same kind of thing. NOTE: ahos:Capability, ahos:Skill, ahos:MCPTool, ahos:Table etc. do name concrete artifacts (e.g. ahos:captureTool), but they describe the contract (what role it plays, what requirement it realises), not the signature. COVERED ELSEWHERE: the codebase itself, grep / IDE navigation, code-level docs where they exist." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

ahos:OperationalInfrastructureBoundary a ahos:Boundary ;
    rdfs:label "Boundary: operational + infrastructure concerns" ;
    rdfs:comment "OUT OF SCOPE: deployments, CI/CD pipelines, hosting, monitoring, secret management at the operational level (the bindings layer is in scope; the actual env-var values aren't), and infrastructure choices that don't shape design. Where Supabase runs, what Vercel does, how GitHub Actions are wired — operational concerns for keeping ahOS running, not design concerns for shaping what ahOS is. NOTE: an infrastructure choice IS in scope when it's load-bearing for design (e.g. ADR-013's choice of Postgres for memory is load-bearing because relational + FTS + jsonb shape the substrate; the specific Supabase instance hosting it is not). COVERED ELSEWHERE: ops runbooks (where they exist), platform consoles directly (Supabase / Vercel / GitHub), and ADRs only for the load-bearing infrastructure decisions." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# --- Known Limitations: in-scope areas where current treatment is incomplete ---
# Sibling section to Boundaries. Each KnownLimitation is something the
# ontology IS supposed to handle but currently handles weakly. Adding
# more as gaps are observed is encouraged.

ahos:TemporalSemanticsLimitation a ahos:KnownLimitation ;
    rdfs:label "Limitation: temporal semantics are shallow" ;
    rdfs:comment "LIMITED: ahos:Period is the only temporal class — it groups facts into named eras (periodMLG, periodCurrent) but provides no instant / interval / duration semantics, no event-time vs valid-time distinction, no temporal-query primitives. The ontology assumes most facts are atemporal, which is wrong for any knowledge whose truth changes over time (state-doc snapshots, capability versions, role-of-substrate over the years). IMPACT: Q2InstanceBoundFolderState (the 'home was MLG, is now somewhere else' problem) is the canonical case where this shows up. Period partly answers Q2, but it's a coarse tool. ACCEPTED BECAUSE: rich temporal modelling (e.g. OWL-Time, allen-interval algebra) is heavy and the active use cases haven't justified it yet; ahkms operates mostly on 'right now' state. WHAT CLOSING WOULD LOOK LIKE: add ahos:Instant, ahos:Interval (or use OWL-Time directly via owl-time prefix), give Period an interval, give state-bound facts a valid-from / valid-to pair, add a temporal-query principle to MCP search." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

ahos:AccessControlLimitation a ahos:KnownLimitation ;
    rdfs:label "Limitation: single-user assumption, no access-control modelling" ;
    rdfs:comment "LIMITED: ahOS is built for a single user (Angus). The closest thing to a scope axis is ahos:Domain (work vs personal), which carves data along role/context lines but is not an access-control mechanism — there's no model of users (ahos:angus is the only Person individual), no permissions, no ACLs, no sharing semantics, no per-capability scope-of-invocation. IMPACT: the moment ahOS is multi-tenant or shared (a team variant of WorkTeamOS, a hosted instance for someone else) this becomes load-bearing — Skill invocations need a calling user, KnowledgeStores need owners, AEL rows need acting-as-whom, the bindings layer needs per-user resolution. ACCEPTED BECAUSE: the single-user assumption is correct today and access modelling is non-trivial. WHAT CLOSING WOULD LOOK LIKE: ahos:User class (separate from ahos:Person — Person is a real human, User is an authenticated identity), permissions modelled as triples (User → Capability with mode), per-store and per-binding ownership/scope, audit events carrying actor." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

ahos:ConfidenceAndUncertaintyLimitation a ahos:KnownLimitation ;
    rdfs:label "Limitation: facts are categorical, no confidence/uncertainty modelling" ;
    rdfs:comment "LIMITED: every ObjectProperty assertion in the ontology is categorical (X has property Y, full stop). The substrate at runtime — particularly LLM-extracted entities and relations from synthesis-time interpretation — produces probabilistic outputs that get coerced into categorical facts. There's no confidence weight on triples, no representation of competing assertions about the same entity (\"the meeting was about Apple\" vs \"the meeting was about UI\" with weights), no contradiction-tolerance for soft conflicts. IMPACT: ahos:SynthesisTimeInterpretation explicitly says interpretation happens at read time and can evolve — that's a probabilistic-by-design pipeline pumping into a categorical substrate, which loses information. Bundling (G2), per-atom inference, entity resolution — all silently throw away uncertainty. ACCEPTED BECAUSE: triple-level confidence weights complicate every query, and a clean v1 substrate is more important than a fully soft one. WHAT CLOSING WOULD LOOK LIKE: ahos:hasConfidence on assertions (RDF reification or named-graphs pattern), a competing-assertions modelling pattern, integration of confidence into ahos:KnowledgeTier (Gold = high confidence, Bronze = needs verification)." ;
    ahos:level "meta" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# --- People ---

ahos:angus a schema:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Angus Hally" ;
    schema:name "Angus Hally" ;
    ahos:level "instance" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# --- The operating system ---

ahos:ahOS a ahos:OperatingSystem ;
    rdfs:label "ahOS" ;
    schema:name "ahOS" ;
    schema:description "Angus Hally's operating system, in a private repo. Built from the initial project knowledgeManagementSystemOct2025 (still in use) and combined with the dotclaude repo." ;
    rdfs:comment "Naming convention [settled 2026-06-15]: 'ahOS' is the product / vision name; 'ahkms' is the codebase package codename (from the original knowledgeMgmtSystemOct2025 repo). No package rename is required or intended — do not conflate the two in design discussions. Level FAQ: ahos:ahOS is tagged ahos:level 'instance' (ABox individual), NOT 'meta'. The ontology is *about* ahOS, which makes ahOS the subject — that's instance. 'meta' is reserved for the ontology's own infrastructure (the ahOntology document node, annotation properties like ahos:level and ahos:inBox). Same pattern as ahos:angus a schema:Person → instance." ;
    schema:url "https://github.com/exampleowner/ahOS" ;          # private repo (resolved 2026-06-16)
    dcterms:creator ahos:angus ;
    schema:dateCreated "2025-10-01"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:dateModified "2026-06-15"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:version "1.0.0" ;
    schema:keywords "operating system", "knowledge management", "private repo", "dotclaude" ;
    schema:license <https://github.com/exampleowner/ahOS/blob/main/LICENSE> ;  # proprietary, all rights reserved (resolved 2026-06-16)
    ahos:level "instance" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# --- Frameworks ---

ahos:PARA a ahos:KnowledgeFramework ;
    rdfs:label "PARA" ;
    rdfs:comment "Organises knowledge by actionability." ;
    ahos:hasCategory ahos:Projects, ahos:Tasks, ahos:Areas, ahos:Resources, ahos:Archive ;
    ahos:level "instance" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

ahos:WorkTeamOS a ahos:KnowledgeFramework ;
    rdfs:label "work-team-os" ;
    rdfs:comment "Classifies team knowledge into work units. Colloquially called 'Work OS' — same thing, just informal naming. Angus has ONE operating system (ahOS); work-team-os is one of the frameworks he uses within it." ;
    ahos:hasCategory ahos:Projects, ahos:Tasks, ahos:Epics,
                     ahos:Documents, ahos:Persons ;
    ahos:level "instance" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# --- Categories: PARA (ordered by actionability) ---

# --- Categories: universal (shared across frameworks) ---

ahos:Projects  a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Projects" ; rdfs:comment "A bounded effort with an outcome. Universal — used by PARA and work-team-os. Domain (work vs personal) carries the framework-affiliation." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .
ahos:Tasks     a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Tasks"    ; rdfs:comment "A unit of work. Universal — used by PARA and work-team-os. Domain carries the framework-affiliation. Design-only — Tasks live in Notion, not entities.entity_type." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# --- Categories: PARA-specific (actionability buckets) ---

ahos:Areas     a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Areas"     ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .
ahos:Resources a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Resources" ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .
ahos:Archive   a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Archive"   ; rdfs:comment "Least actionable. NOT a runtime entity_type: 'archive' was dropped from the entities.entity_type CHECK by migration 067 (#172, 2026-07-07, zero rows) and was redundant with the existing `archived` boolean column (never wired up). Retained here as the PARA 'A' conceptual bucket only." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# --- Categories: PARAMPS-specific (M, P, S — the three letters PARA doesn't carry) ---

ahos:Memory   a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Memory"   ; rdfs:comment "PARAMPS-specific letter (M). Captured knowledge and memories — the long-tail of 'what I know'. Distinct from PARA's actionability buckets: Memory is the substrate-of-record rather than an action queue. Folded from design-ontology branch F (2026-06-15) to complete the PARAMPS 7-letter taxonomy alongside the existing Projects/Areas/Resources/Archive. NOT a runtime entity_type: 'memory' was dropped from the entities.entity_type CHECK by migration 067 (#172, 2026-07-07, zero rows). Retained here as the PARAMPS 'M' conceptual bucket only." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .
ahos:People   a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "People"   ; rdfs:comment "PARAMPS-specific letter (P). Contacts, relationships, individuals — first-class noun in the brainstorm taxonomy. Distinct from Resources (which is about reference material, not people)." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .
ahos:Secure   a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Secure"   ; rdfs:comment "PARAMPS-specific letter (S). Sensitive / credentialed artifacts — separated from the main flow for access-control reasons. Substrate-side this means a different RLS / row-policy treatment, not a different table; the Category lives in the ontology so synthesisers and consumers can see the boundary. NOT a runtime entity_type: 'secure' was dropped from the entities.entity_type CHECK by migration 067 (#172, 2026-07-07, zero rows). Retained here as the PARAMPS 'S' conceptual bucket only." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# --- Categories: work-team-os-specific ---

ahos:Epics     a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Epics"     ; rdfs:comment "Design-only (work-team-os) — no entities.entity_type; lives in Notion." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .
ahos:Documents a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Documents" ; rdfs:comment "Design-only (work-team-os) — no entities.entity_type; lives in Notion." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .
ahos:Persons   a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Persons"   ; rdfs:comment "Design-only (work-team-os naming for people) — the runtime entity_type is 'people'; Persons has no separate entities.entity_type." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# --- Categories: knowledge-graph entity types anchored to external classes ---
# The `concept` entity type (entities.entity_type, added by migration 065 / #249) is
# anchored to skos:Concept in code (ENTITY_TYPE_CLASS_CONTEXT, vocab.ts) — the first
# entity-type→external-class mapping done in code. Recorded here for design-reference
# completeness (#251). Per ADR-033's adopt-direct rule this takes the external
# skos:Concept IRI DIRECTLY — no ahos: proxy and no skos:closeMatch bridge (that bridge
# is only for approximate mints, e.g. ahos:lastEditedBy / ahos:sourceLocation). This
# skos:Concept (a subject / topic / unit-of-thought node in the knowledge graph) is
# distinct from the ahos:DesignConcept class (declared above) for ontology-INTERNAL
# concept nodes (ahos:ScopePredicate, ahos:HouseStyle, ahos:TheEstate) — renamed from the
# clashing ahos:Concept 2026-07-12 (#318 finding 9). NOTE (ADR-036): the adopt-direct
# `skos:Concept a ahos:Category` form here re-types an external class as a local
# individual; under ADR-036 the canonical `concept` term is an ahos: term-node with
# exactMatch → skos:Concept (a mapping, not a re-type). That registry form lands with #326;
# this TTL keeps the adopt-direct form as the interim design-reference. The newer entity types
# (place, event, organization, signal) are now reflected below — anchored to their
# external schema.org classes where an exact match exists (mirroring skos:Concept, per
# ADR-033 adopt-direct), except 'signal' which is app-specific and coined under ahos:.
# Reconciled 2026-07-12 (#319). NOTE: 'arrangement' (ADR-035) is intentionally NOT added
# yet — it needs the entities.entity_type CHECK migration tracked in #327; add its
# Category when that lands.

skos:Concept a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Concept" ; rdfs:comment "The `concept` knowledge-graph entity type (entities.entity_type, migration 065 / #249): a subject / topic / unit-of-thought node. Adopted direct from SKOS per ADR-033 (exact match — the external class IS our meaning), so the external skos:Concept IRI is used with no ahos: proxy and no skos:closeMatch bridge." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

schema:Event a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Event" ; rdfs:comment "The `event` entity_type (entities.entity_type, ADR-025): a dated occurrence (meeting, deadline, milestone) mentioned in a capture. Adopted direct from schema:Event per ADR-033 — exact match, external IRI used with no ahos: proxy." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .
schema:Organization a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Organization" ; rdfs:comment "The `organization` entity_type (entities.entity_type, ADR-025): a company/institution the graph relates people and work to (employed_by / member_of / vendor_for edges presuppose them). Adopted direct from schema:Organization per ADR-033 — exact match." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .
schema:Place a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Place" ; rdfs:comment "The `place` entity_type (entities.entity_type, #188): a location (city, address, venue, region, country); subtype in properties.place_type. Adopted direct from schema:Place per ADR-033 — exact match." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .
ahos:Signal a ahos:Category ; rdfs:label "Signal" ; rdfs:comment "The `signal` entity_type (entities.entity_type, ADR-025): a derived, uncertain inference projected from an artifact (e.g. a tentative scheduling read), carrying confidence in properties. App-specific — no external class matches, so coined under ahos: (no adopt-direct)." ; ahos:level "ontology" ; ahos:inBox "ABox" .

