# C7 — vector store placement: pgvector inside Supabase, or a separate vector DB?

ahos:Q14VectorStorePlacement a ahos:OpenQuestion ;
    rdfs:label "Q14 vector store placement — pgvector in Supabase or separate vector DB?" ;
    rdfs:comment "Brainstorm concept 7 (2026-06-15, line 167): structured (relational) vs unstructured (vector) storage are contrasted as distinct paradigms. ahos:HybridRetrieval assumes FTS + embeddings coexist but does not commit to a topology. Open: (a) embeddings stored in the same Supabase DB via pgvector extension — one substrate, structured schema plus vector index; or (b) a separate specialist vector DB (Pinecone, Weaviate, etc.) — two substrates, separate operational burden. Resolution shapes whether ahos:supabaseDB remains the single substrate or a second ahos:Database instance is needed. ahos:EmbeddingsNotBuiltLimitation defers the implementation; this question defers the architecture." ;
    ahos:about ahos:HybridRetrieval ;
    ahos:status ahos:Open ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# C8 — can dos-inbox immutable-timeline property be preserved in the artifact model?

ahos:Q15ImmutableTimeline a ahos:OpenQuestion ;
    rdfs:label "Q15 immutable capture timeline — can dos-inbox timeline property be preserved in the artifact model?" ;
    rdfs:comment "Brainstorm concept 8 (2026-06-15, line 168): the dos-inbox model's key property was that every capture was an immutable entry on a timeline consumed later. The ahOS artifact model (ahos:Artifact, ahos:Atom) captures the data-nature side but does not formally commit to append-only / immutable semantics on the artifacts table. Open: (a) should ahos:Artifact carry an explicit immutability invariant (no in-place edits, consumption via lifecycle_stage advancement only)? (b) is that invariant already satisfied by the substrate (no UPDATE paths in the MCP tools) without needing to be stated? (c) does the capture-as-event vs artifact-as-data-thing distinction warrant two sibling classes? Likely collapses into ahos:Q1ArtifactDerivedLifecycle if the answer is (b)." ;
    ahos:about ahos:Artifact ;
    ahos:status ahos:Open ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# C9 — Bronze/Silver/Gold promotion: tag-in-place vs promoted-artifact-chain?

ahos:Q16TierPromotion a ahos:OpenQuestion ;
    rdfs:label "Q16 tier promotion — tag-in-place vs promoted-artifact-chain" ;
    rdfs:comment "Brainstorm concept 9 (2026-06-15, line 172): when an artifact rises from Bronze to Silver to Gold, does that look like (a) updating the ahos:tier triple on the existing artifact row (tag in place), or (b) creating a new derived artifact at the higher tier and linking it back via prov:wasDerivedFrom (promoted-and-tagged-up-chain)? Option (a) is simpler but mutates the record; option (b) preserves lineage but multiplies rows. Interacts with ahos:Q15ImmutableTimeline and with how tier-confidence binding would work in practice." ;
    ahos:about ahos:KnowledgeTier ;
    ahos:status ahos:Open ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# C10 — data lake vs data warehouse: does ahOS consciously straddle both?

ahos:Q17DataLakeVsWarehouse a ahos:OpenQuestion ;
    rdfs:label "Q17 data lake vs data warehouse — does ahOS consciously straddle both?" ;
    rdfs:comment "Brainstorm concept 10 (2026-06-15, line 175): the data-lake / data-warehouse distinction maps cleanly onto ahOS — the artifacts table is lake-like (raw bytes, schema-on-read, append-only, no curation at write time); the entities + state docs layer is warehouse-like (processed, structured, synthesised). The brainstorm asks 'Do we have that?' — meaning: is the distinction intentional and modelled, or incidental? Open: (a) should the ontology formally name the lake vs warehouse character of each substrate layer? (b) does ahos:KnowledgeTier map onto this distinction, or are they orthogonal? (c) does recognising the lake layer change retention policy (lake items are never cleaned up; warehouse items supersede them)?" ;
    ahos:about ahos:ArtifactsRegister ;
    ahos:status ahos:Open ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# C11 — files-vs-facts: physical-storage organisation vs semantic-fact organisation

ahos:FilesVsFacts a ahos:Principle ;
    rdfs:label "Files organise physically; facts organise semantically" ;
    rdfs:comment "Brainstorm concept 11 (2026-06-15, line 177): files are physical storage units — they organise by folder hierarchy, naming convention, and capture-path provenance (PARA project, Drive folder, Supabase table row). Facts are semantic claims — they organise by entity type, relationship type, and PARAMPS category regardless of which file they came from. The two layers have different organisational grammars and must not be conflated. Consequence for ahOS: ahos:ArtifactsRegister organises by physical capture provenance; ahos:KnowledgeGraph organises by semantic entity type. Retrieval consumers (ahos:searchKnowledgeTool) should be able to cross the boundary — a query by semantic fact should surface artifacts regardless of which physical store they came from. The principle is the justification for keeping ahos:ArtifactsRegister and ahos:KnowledgeGraph as distinct substrate layers rather than merging them into one table. Likely collapses with ahos:Q1ArtifactDerivedLifecycle and ahos:Q15ImmutableTimeline — design-ontology Phase 2 fold (2026-06-15) flagged these as three names for one underlying tension (artifact-vs-derived-info, capture-event-vs-data-thing, physical-bytes-vs-semantic-facts). The right collapse target depends on which framing is most load-bearing for vision.md; FilesVsFacts is the substrate-layer framing, Q1 is the lifecycle framing, Q15 is the immutability framing." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

ahos:FilesVsFacts ahos:realises ahos:UnifiedKnowledgeManagementSystem .

# End of chunk 4 fold.

# === CHUNK 6 FOLD (2026-06-16) ===
# Dedup-driven fold from docs/sessions/2026-06-15-ahOS-consolidated-mess.md.
# Sections A-O + Vision draft: ~145 candidate items, ~133 already PRESENT in
# the TTL from earlier phases. Two in-place enrichments applied above
# (ahos:R3GuidedStorageAndProcessing comment; ahos:APIPath comment). Four
# new nodes follow. Dropped from the candidate list: ahos:HumanPreviewGate
# (duplicate of existing ahos:HumanPreview at line 2166); ahos:DePrivatizationTax
# (retired terminology — kept as provenance flavour, not promoted to a node).

# F — Scope Predicate (concept): retrieval-time scope filtering, not capture-time stamping.

ahos:ScopePredicate a ahos:DesignConcept ;
    rdfs:label "Scope Predicate" ;
    rdfs:comment "A retrieval-time predicate that filters knowledge by scope (e.g. workspace=work, domain=apple) — applied by Synthesisers when reading atoms from ahos:artifactsRegister, not stamped at capture. Realises ahos:SynthesisTimeInterpretation on the access side: scope is a read-time lens over the single atomic substrate (ahos:SingleAtomicSubstrate), not a partition on the substrate itself. Distinct from ahos:Domain (which is one *value* a scope predicate may take); distinct from ahos:bindingScope (which is a deployment-time binding context, not a query-time filter). Folded from consolidated-mess section F (concepts list, 2026-06-15)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# G — workspace-cache write-back: open question, ADR-018 future enhancements.

ahos:Q18WorkspaceCacheWriteback a ahos:OpenQuestion ;
    rdfs:label "Q18 workspace-cache write-back — cache synthesis inference or re-infer each read?" ;
    rdfs:comment "Consolidated-mess section G open (2026-06-15): does the substrate persist a previous Synthesiser's per-atom workspace/type/domain inference (as a cache, queryable across consumers), or re-infer at every read? ADR-018 §4 leaves this open under future enhancements. ahos:SingleAtomicSubstrate currently states there is no per-consumer cache or projection — but that is a description of today, not a closed decision. Resolution shapes (a) whether ahos:artifactsRegister grows a workspace_cache or annotations table; (b) the freshness contract — does a cache invalidate when interpretation rules evolve, defeating the point of synthesis-time inference; (c) the consistency contract — if two Synthesisers infer different workspaces for the same atom, which wins. Pointed at ahos:SynthesisTimeInterpretation because that's the principle whose operational economics the answer chooses." ;
    ahos:about ahos:SynthesisTimeInterpretation ;
    ahos:status ahos:Open ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# I — House style (concept): voice/style guide applied by correspondence skill.

ahos:HouseStyle a ahos:DesignConcept ;
    rdfs:label "House style" ;
    rdfs:comment "The writing voice and style guide the correspondence skill applies when drafting outbound messages. Distinct from ahos:SendTool mechanics (which handle dispatch, idempotency, provenance) — house-style is the *judgment* side of the correspondence edge, send-tools are the *mechanics* side. Realises ahos:BuildOpinionsRentMechanics at the edge: the opinion is house-style (built and owned by ahOS), the mechanics are the Composio-rented send paths. Folded from consolidated-mess section I (ADR-017 §3, 2026-06-15)." ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# I — Open: is correspondence one example of a generalised consumer-edge pattern?

ahos:Q19CorrespondenceEdgePattern a ahos:OpenQuestion ;
    rdfs:label "Q19 correspondence-edge as exemplar — does the pattern generalise to other consumers?" ;
    rdfs:comment "Consolidated-mess section I open (2026-06-15): is correspondence one instance of a generalised consumer-of-substrate edge pattern (investor-brief, weekly-reflection, retention-tracker, customer-comms timeline would all follow the same ahos:KnowledgeGrounded + ahos:DeletableEdgeRule contract), or is correspondence structurally distinct because it *sends outbound* through rented mechanics while other consumers produce *internal* artifacts? Resolution decides (a) whether ahos:DeletableEdgeRule generalises across all consumers or stays correspondence-specific; (b) whether ahos:HouseStyle has analogues for each consumer (each gets its own opinion-layer); (c) whether ahos:HumanPreview is correspondence-only (because of outbound side-effects) or applies to all edges that leave the trusted boundary. Pointed at ahos:KnowledgeGrounded because that's the principle whose reach the answer defines." ;
    ahos:about ahos:KnowledgeGrounded ;
    ahos:status ahos:Open ;
    ahos:level "ontology" ;
    ahos:inBox "ABox" .

# End of chunk 6 fold.

